WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.730
How should AI Agents research in parallel?

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.288
First, test task fit.

3
00:00:04.288 --> 00:00:07.680
Anthropic draws a useful boundary in its multi-agent research retrospective.

4
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.276
Parallelism expands search breadth, but the coordination cost is worthwhile

5
00:00:11.276 --> 00:00:14.920
only when independent paths, a valuable result, and enough room for tool use

6
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:15.128
appear together.

7
00:00:15.128 --> 00:00:18.979
In Anthropic’s internal research evaluation, an Opus 4 lead agent with

8
00:00:18.979 --> 00:00:21.672
Sonnet 4 subagents outperformed a single Opus 4 agent by 90.

9
00:00:21.672 --> 00:00:22.411
2 percent.

10
00:00:22.411 --> 00:00:26.064
That number belongs to one internal evaluation, not a promise for every

11
00:00:26.064 --> 00:00:26.325
In this episode, we will build a single-versus-multi-agent decision tree.

12
00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:26.601
task.

13
00:00:29.305 --> 00:00:33.397
We will test fit, define delegation, size the search, evaluate evidence, and

14
00:00:33.397 --> 00:00:35.674
add the recovery controls a production system needs.

15
00:00:35.674 --> 00:00:39.031
This video is packed with practical value to help you become better at using

16
00:00:39.031 --> 00:00:39.501
It is a longer episode, so follow and save it for later.

17
00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:39.652
AI.

18
00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:43.454
The first move is not adding agents.

19
00:00:43.454 --> 00:00:46.913
It is deciding whether the problem contains independent exploration paths

20
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:47.661
that deserve parallel work.

21
00:00:47.661 --> 00:00:50.720
Research often fits because an answer may require several directions.

22
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.421
Market, technology, user, and risk research can proceed separately before

23
00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:56.452
the lead agent compresses them into one conclusion.

24
00:00:56.452 --> 00:01:00.367
Subagents also help when one context window would otherwise hold too many

25
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:02.252
sources, tool results, and working hypotheses.

26
00:01:02.252 --> 00:01:05.212
Each path can return a compact, high-signal research summary.

27
00:01:05.212 --> 00:01:08.654
But decomposition creates synchronization when every step depends on the

28
00:01:08.654 --> 00:01:10.072
exact state produced by the previous step.

29
00:01:10.072 --> 00:01:14.189
Many coding tasks share one codebase and test state, so one continuous agent

30
00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:14.464
may fit better.

31
00:01:14.464 --> 00:01:15.660
Economics matters too.

32
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.362
In Anthropic’s usage data, agents used about four times the tokens of chat

33
00:01:19.362 --> 00:01:22.487
interactions, while multi-agent systems used about fifteen times as many.

34
00:01:22.487 --> 00:01:25.748
That makes multi-agent work sensible for valuable outcomes, expensive

35
00:01:25.748 --> 00:01:28.691
mistakes, or questions where search breadth can materially change the

36
00:01:28.691 --> 00:01:29.009
It should not add ceremony to a simple request.

37
00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:29.340
answer.

38
00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:32.502
Turn the decision into three gates.

39
00:01:32.502 --> 00:01:33.838
Can paths run independently?

40
00:01:33.838 --> 00:01:35.546
Is one context becoming overloaded?

41
00:01:35.546 --> 00:01:37.585
Can the value of the result cover the extra cost?

42
00:01:37.585 --> 00:01:40.340
The more gates pass, the stronger the case for parallelism.

43
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:41.196
Chapter recap.

44
00:01:41.196 --> 00:01:43.605
First, look for paths that can progress independently.

45
00:01:43.605 --> 00:01:46.649
Second, be cautious when the task depends on heavily shared state.

46
00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:49.504
Third, reserve the higher cost for high-value outcomes.

47
00:01:49.504 --> 00:01:52.397
After the fit gate, the lead agent’s main job is not searching.

48
00:01:52.397 --> 00:01:55.942
It is dividing the question into assignments that do not overlap and do not

49
00:01:55.942 --> 00:01:55.967
Every delegation should name an objective and the expected output.

50
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:56.491
leave gaps.

51
00:01:58.648 --> 00:02:02.699
Instead of saying research competitors, ask for pricing, target users, and

52
00:02:02.699 --> 00:02:04.578
verifiable sources for three named products.

53
00:02:04.578 --> 00:02:06.784
Then declare the allowed tools and source boundary.

54
00:02:06.784 --> 00:02:10.645
Naming search, browsing, or databases and prioritizing primary sources keeps

55
00:02:10.645 --> 00:02:12.470
subagents from wandering through weak material.

56
00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.305
Tell each subagent what not to do as well.

57
00:02:14.305 --> 00:02:18.032
Covered regions, time ranges, and result formats should be explicit, or

58
00:02:18.032 --> 00:02:19.807
several agents may return the same evidence.

59
00:02:19.807 --> 00:02:23.685
Anthropic describes effective delegation through the objective, output

60
00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:25.529
format, tools, sources, and task boundaries.

61
00:02:25.529 --> 00:02:28.782
The lead agent should still revise assignments when the first findings

62
00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:28.946
change the map.

63
00:02:28.946 --> 00:02:31.214
Broad first and narrow second is often effective.

64
00:02:31.214 --> 00:02:34.981
Run a parallel scan for leads, then deepen only the branches that can change

65
00:02:34.981 --> 00:02:35.402
the final judgment.

66
00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:36.788
Collect more than conclusions.

67
00:02:36.788 --> 00:02:40.290
Require evidence links, important uncertainties, and the reason a path

68
00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.313
stopped, so the lead agent can decide which findings are safe to merge.

69
00:02:43.313 --> 00:02:44.166
Chapter recap.

70
00:02:44.166 --> 00:02:46.491
First, make the objective and output explicit.

71
00:02:46.491 --> 00:02:49.845
Second, specify sources, tools, and boundaries together.

72
00:02:49.845 --> 00:02:53.027
Third, collect evidence and uncertainty, not conclusions alone.

73
00:02:53.027 --> 00:02:55.061
Multi-agent does not mean more is always better.

74
00:02:55.061 --> 00:02:58.332
Search effort should scale with both question complexity and expected value.

75
00:02:58.332 --> 00:03:02.357
Anthropic’s examples suggest that a simple fact may need one agent and three

76
00:03:02.357 --> 00:03:02.702
to ten tool calls.

77
00:03:02.702 --> 00:03:05.160
That scale does not require another coordination layer.

78
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.030
A comparison across several targets may use two to four agents, with roughly

79
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.496
ten to fifteen tool calls per agent, so each research path reaches useful

80
00:03:12.496 --> 00:03:12.747
Complex research may use more than ten subagents, but that is not a default.

81
00:03:12.747 --> 00:03:13.052
depth.

82
00:03:15.896 --> 00:03:20.179
The lead agent must see progress, detect duplication, and stop when marginal

83
00:03:20.179 --> 00:03:20.316
value falls.

84
00:03:20.316 --> 00:03:23.088
Tool calls can also run in parallel inside one subagent.

85
00:03:23.088 --> 00:03:26.646
Anthropic suggests that complex paths often use three to five subagents,

86
00:03:26.646 --> 00:03:28.879
with three or more tools running concurrently per agent.

87
00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:32.811
When paths are truly independent, parallel work can cut time by as much as

88
00:03:32.811 --> 00:03:33.791
90 percent on complex queries.

89
00:03:33.791 --> 00:03:37.424
The gain comes from overlapping waits, not from making each agent inherently

90
00:03:37.424 --> 00:03:37.591
Give the decision tree three effort tiers: light, comparison, and deep

91
00:03:37.591 --> 00:03:37.991
smarter.

92
00:03:41.331 --> 00:03:41.410
Each tier defines agent count, tool budget, stopping conditions, and the

93
00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:41.922
research.

94
00:03:45.259 --> 00:03:46.376
evidence required to escalate.

95
00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:47.239
Chapter recap.

96
00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:49.706
First, choose an effort tier from task complexity.

97
00:03:49.706 --> 00:03:52.773
Second, parallel gains come from overlapping independent waits.

98
00:03:52.773 --> 00:03:55.687
Third, stop expanding when marginal value starts to fall.

99
00:03:55.687 --> 00:04:00.070
Multi-agent paths change dynamically, so evaluation cannot require one fixed

100
00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:00.582
sequence of steps.

101
00:04:00.582 --> 00:04:01.597
Start with the result.

102
00:04:01.597 --> 00:04:02.591
Are the facts correct?

103
00:04:02.591 --> 00:04:04.127
Do citations support the claims?

104
00:04:04.127 --> 00:04:05.093
Is the question covered?

105
00:04:05.093 --> 00:04:07.113
Are the sources authoritative enough for the decision?

106
00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.022
Then inspect whether the process was reasonable.

107
00:04:09.022 --> 00:04:11.137
Did the decomposition leave gaps or duplication?

108
00:04:11.137 --> 00:04:12.227
Were tool calls useful?

109
00:04:12.227 --> 00:04:14.752
Did the lead agent revise its plan when new evidence appeared?

110
00:04:14.752 --> 00:04:19.035
Anthropic uses small-sample human review and criteria for factual accuracy,

111
00:04:19.035 --> 00:04:21.937
citations, completeness, source quality, and tool efficiency.

112
00:04:21.937 --> 00:04:24.473
The point is to create quality signals people can explain.

113
00:04:24.473 --> 00:04:28.105
If you score only the final answer, a system may get lucky through expensive

114
00:04:28.105 --> 00:04:28.218
If you demand one fixed trace, you punish reasonable paths that react to

115
00:04:28.218 --> 00:04:28.711
searching.

116
00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:32.017
A stronger design separates outcome standards from process guardrails.

117
00:04:32.017 --> 00:04:32.506
evidence.

118
00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:38.802
The outcome must pass, while the path may vary as long as it leaves evidence

119
00:04:38.802 --> 00:04:39.645
that a reviewer can inspect.

120
00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.823
Start with ten real questions for your own system.

121
00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:45.709
Compare a single-agent baseline, answer quality, tokens, latency, and

122
00:04:45.709 --> 00:04:47.725
failure types before expanding parallel work.

123
00:04:47.725 --> 00:04:48.589
Chapter recap.

124
00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:51.720
First, evaluate facts, citations, and coverage.

125
00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.083
Second, allow dynamic paths but require an explainable process.

126
00:04:55.083 --> 00:04:58.213
Third, use a real baseline to decide whether parallelism pays.

127
00:04:58.213 --> 00:05:01.804
A research demo that finishes once is not yet a multi-agent system that can

128
00:05:01.804 --> 00:05:02.736
recover reliably in production.

129
00:05:02.736 --> 00:05:06.059
Any subagent can time out, lose a tool, or return an empty result.

130
00:05:06.059 --> 00:05:09.674
Persist task state so a failure reruns only the affected path instead of

131
00:05:09.674 --> 00:05:10.419
restarting the whole tree.

132
00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:14.516
A useful checkpoint records the assignment, owner, current state, evidence

133
00:05:14.516 --> 00:05:15.358
location, and next action.

134
00:05:15.358 --> 00:05:19.309
After a process restart, the system can resume without treating old output

135
00:05:19.309 --> 00:05:19.628
as new evidence.

136
00:05:19.628 --> 00:05:21.373
Observability must cross the whole tree.

137
00:05:21.373 --> 00:05:25.119
Trace every delegation, tool call, retry, and synthesis step so you can

138
00:05:25.119 --> 00:05:28.149
distinguish search failure, coordination failure, and merge failure.

139
00:05:28.149 --> 00:05:29.606
Deployment should be gradual too.

140
00:05:29.606 --> 00:05:32.819
Anthropic describes rainbow deployments that keep old and new versions

141
00:05:32.819 --> 00:05:35.401
available so agents already running are not broken by a code change.

142
00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.970
The current architecture also has a synchronous bottleneck: the lead agent

143
00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.344
waits for all subagents before moving on.

144
00:05:40.344 --> 00:05:44.033
Async work can be faster, but it adds scheduling, concurrency, and

145
00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:44.965
result-merging complexity.

146
00:05:44.965 --> 00:05:48.969
The production checklist is persistent state, path-level retries, end-to-end

147
00:05:48.969 --> 00:05:51.377
tracing, gradual deployment, and explicit stopping rules.

148
00:05:51.377 --> 00:05:54.085
Speed is an outcome of reliability, not the only goal.

149
00:05:54.085 --> 00:05:54.933
Chapter recap.

150
00:05:54.933 --> 00:05:57.653
First, persist state and evidence outside the process.

151
00:05:57.653 --> 00:06:00.844
Second, retry only the failed path and preserve the full trace.

152
00:06:00.844 --> 00:06:04.123
Third, protect production with gradual releases and stopping rules.

153
00:06:04.123 --> 00:06:05.455
Now connect the decision tree.

154
00:06:05.455 --> 00:06:09.143
Ask whether paths are independent, whether one context is overloaded, and

155
00:06:09.143 --> 00:06:10.571
whether the result can justify the cost.

156
00:06:10.571 --> 00:06:14.480
Only then move into delegation, effort sizing, evaluation, and recovery.

157
00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.891
Keep a single agent when the task depends on shared state or one agent

158
00:06:17.891 --> 00:06:18.763
already completes it reliably.

159
00:06:18.763 --> 00:06:21.738
Multi-agent is a task architecture, not a badge of capability.

160
00:06:21.738 --> 00:06:24.678
Before your next deep-research job, draw three candidate paths.

161
00:06:24.678 --> 00:06:28.359
Give each one an objective, source boundary, output, and stopping condition.

162
00:06:28.359 --> 00:06:31.170
Run them in parallel only if they can truly progress independently.

163
00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.767
Follow Tiny Agent and become better at using AI!
